\description{
Identifies pregnancies, their outcomes and their start dates in the Medical Birth Registry of
Norway (MBRN), the Norwegian Patient Registry (NPR) and the primary-care claims database
(KUHR), with the UiO pregnancy algorithm (Nordeng et al. 2024).
}
\section{How the algorithm works}{

\enumerate{
\item \strong{MBRN.} Each child record gets an outcome from the vital-status variable \code{DODKAT}:
live birth (0-6, 11-12), fetal death (7-9; stillbirth or miscarriage, see
\code{stillbirth_min_days}), induced abortion (10) or unknown (13; a live birth if the 5-minute
Apgar score is above 0). Children of one mother with starts less than 13 days apart, or the
same birth date, form one pregnancy; multiple pregnancies with discordant outcomes get one
outcome (an induced abortion of any fetus gives an induced abortion; a stillbirth together
with live births or miscarriages gives a stillbirth; live births and miscarriages give a
miscarriage). Records without the mother's ID or gestational age are dropped. The start is
the birth date minus the recorded gestational age in days (\code{SVLEN_DG}).
\item \strong{NPR.} Records with O00 (ectopic), O01 (molar), O02-O03 (miscarriage) or O04 (induced
abortion) are outcomes. On one day, molar > ectopic > miscarriage > induced abortion.
Repeated records of one event (less than \code{same_event_days} apart) are merged, and records on
the date of an MBRN outcome are linked to it. Records less than \code{same_event_days} after an
MBRN outcome, or between the start and the end of an MBRN pregnancy, are dropped.
\item \strong{KUHR.} ICD-10 O02-O03 and ICPC-2 W82 records are miscarriages, merged and dropped by
the same rules, and also dropped when they are less than \code{same_event_days} before or after
an MBRN or NPR non-live outcome. KUHR adds miscarriages only.
\item \strong{Dating.} Pregnancies with MBRN gestational age keep the MBRN start. The others are
dated, in this order: (a) induced abortions with an O35 code recorded within
\code{o35_window_days} after the abortion get \code{o35_induced_days}; (b) pregnancy markers, NPR
first, then KUHR (see \code{\link[=uio_hospital_codes]{uio_hospital_codes()}}): start = marker date minus the marker's days,
for the first marker in priority order that is recorded within \code{marker_lookback_days} (and
\code{marker_rejection_days}) before the outcome and implies a length below
\code{marker_rejection_days}; (c) if markers were found but
all were rejected, \code{marker_rejection_days}; (d) otherwise the outcome-specific default
(\code{induced_abortion_default_days}, \code{miscarriage_default_days}, \code{molar_default_days},
\code{ectopic_default_days}, or a draw from \code{induced_abortion_default_distribution}). A start
before the woman's previous outcome is moved to that outcome date
(\code{truncated_at_previous_outcome}).
}
}

\section{Time conventions}{

The start date is \strong{day 0}; the gestational age at the outcome is
\code{GA_event_days_final = date_outcome - pr_start_final} (days) and
\code{GA_event_week_final = floor(GA_event_days_final / 7)} (completed weeks). A default of 56 days
therefore puts the outcome at 8+0 weeks, which the Norwegian registries count as week 9
(ordinal weeks: week \code{n} = days \code{7(n-1)} to \verb{7n-1}). All windows are in whole days; "within
\code{d} days before the outcome" includes the outcome date and the day \code{d} days before it.
}

\section{Settings}{

Every setting is an argument with a default (the main analysis of the four-algorithm
comparison). They can also be passed together as a named list in \code{settings}, for example
\code{settings = uio_v13_settings()} for the rules of version 1.3. A setting may be given either as an
argument or in \code{settings}, not both. Details of each setting: \code{\link[=uio_default_settings]{uio_default_settings()}}.
}

\section{Cached files}{

The source extracts are read once and cached in \code{workdir} (\code{npr_mothers_full.csv},
\code{birth_registry_full.csv}, \code{hospital_full.csv}, \code{primary_care_full.csv}); later runs read the
cached files. Delete them to read the extracts again, for example after changing
\code{source_files}. Settings never change the cached files, except that with
\code{stillbirth_min_weight_g} a cached \code{birth_registry_full.csv} without the birth weight is read
again from the source and cached with it.

Three small C++ helpers are compiled with \code{\link[Rcpp:sourceCpp]{Rcpp::sourceCpp()}} when the algorithm runs, so a
C++ compiler is needed at run time (Rtools on Windows).
